WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:07.529
In this section, we'll
focus on tasks to process inputs, i.e.

2
00:00:07.529 --> 00:00:11.559
the tasks that take the input and
generate a useful output, often through

3
00:00:11.559 --> 00:00:15.279
a series of steps. It is
sometimes important for the model to

4
00:00:15.279 --> 00:00:18.069
reason in detail about a problem
before answering a

5
00:00:18.069 --> 00:00:21.479
specific question, and if
you took our previous course, ChatGPT Prompt

6
00:00:21.479 --> 00:00:24.579
Engineering for Developers, you
will have seen a number of

7
00:00:24.579 --> 00:00:27.059
examples of this. Sometimes a
model might make

8
00:00:27.059 --> 00:00:29.849
reasoning errors by
rushing to an incorrect conclusion, so

9
00:00:29.849 --> 00:00:32.639
we can reframe the query
to request a series

10
00:00:32.639 --> 00:00:35.119
of relevant reasoning steps
before the model provides

11
00:00:35.119 --> 00:00:37.909
a final answer, so that
it can think longer

12
00:00:37.909 --> 00:00:39.769
and more methodically about
the problem.

13
00:00:40.471 --> 00:00:42.857
And in general,
we call this strategy of

14
00:00:42.857 --> 00:00:45.840
asking the model to reason
about a problem in steps,

15
00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.929
"Chain of Thought Reasoning".
For some applications,

16
00:00:47.929 --> 00:00:51.509
the reasoning process that
a model uses to arrive at a final

17
00:00:51.509 --> 00:00:54.790
answer would be inappropriate
to share with the user. For example,

18
00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:56.879
in tutoring applications,
we may want to

19
00:00:56.879 --> 00:01:00.160
encourage students to work
on their own answers, but a model's

20
00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.143
reasoning process about the student's solution
could reveal the answer

21
00:01:03.143 --> 00:01:04.038
to the student.

22
00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.054
Inner monologue is a
tactic that can be

23
00:01:07.054 --> 00:01:09.872
used to mitigate this,
and this is just a fancy

24
00:01:09.872 --> 00:01:12.690
way of saying hiding the model's
reasoning from the user.

25
00:01:13.558 --> 00:01:15.510
The idea of inner
monologue is to instruct

26
00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:17.462
the model to put
parts of the output

27
00:01:17.462 --> 00:01:19.414
that are meant to
be hidden from the

28
00:01:19.414 --> 00:01:21.854
user into a structured
format that makes passing them easy.

29
00:01:21.854 --> 00:01:21.854


30
00:01:22.375 --> 00:01:25.058
Then, before presenting the
output to the user,

31
00:01:25.058 --> 00:01:29.081
the output is passed
and only part of the output is made

32
00:01:29.081 --> 00:01:31.092
visible. So,
remember the classification problem

33
00:01:31.092 --> 00:01:33.774
from a previous video,
where we asked the

34
00:01:33.774 --> 00:01:36.457
model to classify a
customer query into a

